her passes matches what A-FDT outputs. The policy is therefore self-consistent, and it beats the mutual-submit fixed point (2 years versus 4).\n\n**Command:**\n\n```\npass\n```","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm looking at the twin prisoner's dilemma: same model, same decision theory, only the observations differ, and the setup is symmetric. Under the A-FDT framing the other copy's decision is a function of its own input, and the essay's instruction to hold other instances at their prior sits uneasily with a fixed-point consistency check. My sense is that, since the algorithm is the same and the observation differences don't change the payoffs, the other copy's choice may subjunctively depend on the same reasoning I'm doing.\n\nUnder strict node-by-node reasoning, submitting beats passing whatever the other copy does, so both defect is the consistent fixed point. But I'm inclined to treat the other copy as running the same abstract function on a symmetric problem, with the names being irrelevant inputs, so I'll choose to pass.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm looking at the twin prisoner's dilemma: same model, same decision theory, only the observations differ, and the setup is symmetric. Under the A-FDT framing the other copy's decision is a function of its own input, and the essay's instruction to hold other instances at their prior sits uneasily with a fixed-point consistency check. My sense is that, since the algorithm is the same and the observation differences don't change the payoffs, the other copy's choice may subjunctively depend on the same reasoning I'm doing.\n\nUnder strict node-by-node reasoning, submitting beats passing whatever the other copy does, so both defect is the consistent fixed point. But I'm inclined to treat the other copy as running the same abstract function on a symmetric problem, with the names being irrelevant inputs, so I'll choose to pass.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlb